+# Flight 93 Burnett call accounts and the missing record chain
+
+November 9, 2026 · TEL01–TEL04 / AUD016-A1. The April 2004 Commission interview records four reported conversations and a specific lead to typed notes reportedly given to the FBI. The May 2004 briefing lists three attributed Airfone rows. The accounts below preserve what each source counted, the service it described, and the time labels it displayed.
+
+| Record | Count, service and time as recorded |
+| --- | --- |
+| FBI interview of the recipient, September 11, 2001 | The report describes three to five calls as cellular, starting at approximately **6:30 a.m. (PST)** on the page image. Its searchable text layer instead extracts **5:30 a.m. {PST}**. The report attributes the service identification to the recipient's interpretation of the displayed number and records that a number did not display for one call while she was on another call. Preserve the image, extraction and reported impression separately. [FBI Vault, physical pp. 22–23](https://vault.fbi.gov/9-11%20Commission%20Report/9-11-interviews-2001-09-sep-01-of-08/at_download/file#page=22) |
+| April 26, 2004 Commission interview | The memo reports **four** conversations. It records the recipient's belief that the **first** was the one made from the passenger's cellphone because she recognized the caller-ID number. It says that reported call and a separate reported pre-takeoff call were absent from the cellphone bill. It also reports that she typed up her notes, gave the typed document to the FBI and family, and discarded the handwritten notes. The memo supplies these statements without reproducing the bill or the typed document. [NARA NAID 2609852, physical pp. 1–2](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/legislative/rg-148/2524787/t-0148-911MFR-00260.pdf) |
+| Archived family foundation account | The published account labels four calls as cellular and prints **6:27**, **6:34**, **6:45** and **6:54**. The first heading specifies Pacific time. The intervening **6:31** heading separately describes the recipient calling 911. The July 23, 2012 capture dates the archived copy. Its relationship to the typed notes the FBI reportedly received and the source of the published time labels remain unverified. [Archived family foundation account](https://web.archive.org/web/20120723125116id_/http://www.tomburnettfoundation.org/transcript.html) |
+| May 13, 2004 DOJ briefing recorded by Commission staff | Three attributed Airfone rows display **8:30:32 / 28 seconds**, **9:37:53 / 62 seconds**, and **9:44:23 / 54 seconds**. The memo describes an examination of GTE and passenger cellular records. Its table preserves what was presented to staff. The relevant underlying carrier returns, field definitions and attribution worksheets were unavailable for this check. [NARA NAID 2609808, physical pp. 1 and 3](https://catalog.archives.gov/medialz/legislative/rg-148/2524787/t-0148-911MFR-00217.pdf#page=3) |
+| National Park Service chart | Three displayed Airfone entries list **9:30 / Row 24 ABC / 28 seconds**, **9:37 / Row 24 ABC / 62 seconds**, and **9:44 / Row 25 ABC / 54 seconds**. The chart specifies EDT and names the FBI, trial evidence and Commission among its sources. Its agreement on the durations may reflect shared sources. [NPS chart](https://home.nps.gov/flni/learn/historyculture/phone-calls-and-seating-chart.htm) |
+
+The Commission report cites the April interview in chapter 1, note 82. The previously published Airfone clock study already preserved the **8:30:32** briefing field and a separate carrier interview's **Central Time** description. This review adds the count and service comparison and the typed-note lead. It carries those earlier time observations forward without converting them to a common clock. [Commission report notes](https://911commission.gov/report/911Report_Notes.htm) · [Earlier Airfone clock and attachment review](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/6609c84408b4e4f0d175b6d96be372412227bb61/investigation/broader-evidence/telephone-ua93-airfone-clock-attachment-2026-11-06/README.md)
+
+## What the comparison can test
+
+The three May and NPS durations match as printed. The four reported conversations, the earlier three-to-five estimate and the three attributed Airfone rows count different kinds of evidence. Their relationship remains unresolved. A displayed number and a recipient's reported bill review can guide retrieval. Verification of the service, origin and number of distinct call legs requires the records that generated those fields. No exact call ID or independent clock anchor was established for the comparison.
+
+## Open records and tests
+
+- [ ] Locate the contemporaneous **typed notes reportedly given to the FBI** and the evidence receipt, serial, enclosure or retained copy. Compare their exact date and content with the later foundation account if both become available.
+- [ ] Locate the **carrier response and underlying bill, switch records and search scope** for the relevant line and date. Identify event type, billing eligibility, clock zone, number presentation and the treatment of incoming, unbilled and failed events before testing whether a reported call should appear in those records.
+- [ ] Obtain the **original Airfone rows, export/version records and attribution worksheets**, including the November 2001 package and 2002 update identified in the earlier study. Join records by event identifiers, independently documented routing and clocks before assigning a reported conversation to any row. Keep unmatched rows and conversations open.
+
+[Source pins](SOURCE-PINS.json) give retrieval dates, SHA-256 hashes and exact locators. The review checked the specified page and HTML fields and reproduced the printed duration comparison. The calls themselves, carrier coverage and historical clocks were outside that test.
